# PREPENDED, NOT APPENDED, AND THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. Later lines win, so
# a trailing `*` rule silently destroys every pin below it. Measured, not
# reasoned: appending flips co_retail_hosts_block.txt from `text: unset` to
# `text: auto`, unpinning it; prepending leaves it byte-identical (142 CRLF
# lines, same md5). Why this exists at all: `core.autocrlf` is per-machine and
# unversioned, and this box carries 19 separate clones of this repo -- a
# setting each of them must remember is the kind of rule that decays.
* text=auto eol=lf

co_retail_hosts_block.txt -text

# `tools/build_addon.py` GENERATES this tree and writes it LF (see `_write_lf`
# and `tests/test_build_addon_eol.py`). Without these two lines the build's
# output and the checkout disagree under every `core.autocrlf` setting, and the
# disagreement is invisible on exactly the machine that produces it:
#   autocrlf=false -- 17 files really differ, 11,892 insertions and 11,892
#                     deletions, nothing surviving `--ignore-cr-at-eol`
#   autocrlf=true  -- 17 files read MODIFIED with an EMPTY patch, because the
#                     expected checked-out form is CRLF and the build wrote LF
# Nothing else pinned them: `git check-attr text eol` answered `unspecified`.
blender/io_scene_c3/vendor/** text eol=lf
blender/io_scene_c3/blender_manifest.toml text eol=lf
